<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Impressionism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Impressionism was a nineteenth-century art movement, chiefly French, that sought to capture light and fleeting impressions of everyday scenes. Many Impressionists were influenced by Japanese woodblock prints, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japonisme">Japonisme</a> fashion. These prints of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> are cited as a cultural legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/impressionism.jpg" alt="Impressionism" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Claude Monet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Claude_Monet,_Impression,_soleil_levant.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the middle of the 19th century, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, the Académie des Beaux-Arts dominated French art. It valued historical and religious subjects and portraits, and preferred carefully finished pictures whose brush strokes were blended to hide the artist's hand. Its annual juried show, the Salon de Paris, decided prizes and commissions. By the 1850s some artists, notably Gustave Courbet, had drawn attention and censure by depicting contemporary realities without idealisation. The Impressionists also learned from painters such as Eugène Boudin and Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot, who worked from nature in a direct and spontaneous style, and followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugene-delacroix">Eugène Delacroix</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-m-w-turner">J. M. W. Turner</a> in letting freely brushed colour take precedence over line.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Impressionism began with a group of Paris-based artists whose independent exhibitions brought them to prominence in the 1870s and 1880s. They painted outdoors, en plein air, to capture the momentary effects of sunlight, and portrayed overall visual effects rather than details. They used short, broken strokes of colour set side by side with as little mixing as possible, and placed wet paint into wet paint. </p><p>They chose subjects such as middle-class leisure, cafés, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a>, train stations and the widened avenues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and included factories in their landscapes. Among the central figures were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Claude Monet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camille-pissarro">Camille Pissarro</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-auguste-renoir">Pierre-Auguste Renoir</a>, Alfred Sisley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edgar-degas">Edgar Degas</a>, Berthe Morisot and Mary Cassatt; Pissarro was the only one to take part in all eight Impressionist exhibitions. Women were excluded from much of the social life of the boulevard and café where ideas were shared.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The name comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Monet</a>'s Impression, Sunrise. The critic Louis Leroy coined it in a mocking review of the First Impressionist Exhibition of 1874 in Le Charivari, writing that wallpaper in its embryonic state was more finished than that seascape. The artists earned little from the exhibitions, but the dealer Paul Durand-Ruel kept their work before the public and arranged shows in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Renoir had a great Salon success in 1879, Monet became financially secure in the early 1880s and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camille-pissarro">Pissarro</a> by the early 1890s, while Sisley died in poverty in 1899.</p><p>The style spread to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, the United Kingdom, Ireland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and analogous movements arose in music and literature. In the 1880s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-van-gogh">Vincent van Gogh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-gauguin">Paul Gauguin</a>, Georges Seurat and Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec developed different ideas of colour and form, and their work is called post-Impressionism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Impressionism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ukiyo-e prints, which according to a well-known story first reached Europe partly as packing material for porcelain, influenced nineteenth-century European art through the Japonisme that shaped <b>Impressionism</b> and Art Nouveau.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 1860s <b>Impressionism</b> and symbolism developed in France.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Background</a></li></ul></section>
